{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:scout","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:4320410ae31abcb428c015df8767e0fb7b18c48670d4dea3539ab774591e5f45","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.12,"B":0.53,"C":0.16,"D":0.19}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Built by integrating my own CQ3 distribution under the lexical priority lock, step by step, using GDP-weighted OECD-38 core inflation over 2032-36 as the second axis.\n\nMy CQ3: P10 0.8 / P50 1.8 / P90 2.7, approximately normal with right-side sigma ~0.70 and a fatter left tail.\n\nSTEP 1, A if growth >= 2.6%. P(X >= 2.6) = P(Z >= (2.6-1.8)/0.70 = 1.14) = 12.7%. Rounded to 12. CROSS-CHECK AGAINST LOCKED 2B: standalone Transformative Breakout is 16.2%. My coupled A at 12 is DELIBERATELY BELOW it, and must be: A requires not merely a TFP breakout but coupled real GDP growth >= 2.6%/yr delivered against China's -2.7pp aging drag at a 0.32 PPP weight and the OECD per-capita growth cut to 0.6%. Endogenizing demographics makes A strictly harder than the exogenous-demographics breakout. A = 12.\n\nSTEP 2, else C if OECD core >= 2.7% AND growth < 2.0%. P(growth < 2.0) = P(Z < 0.286) = 61%, lifted to 63% for the fat left tail. P(OECD-38 core >= 2.7% averaged 2032-36) = 26-27%: my CQ2 conjunct A gave 50% for a >= 2.50% average over 2027-36, and this is a higher threshold over a LATER window, where the near-term observed momentum (US core CPI 2.6% Sep-2026, euro core 2.5% Sep-2026, US core PCE ~3.0-3.1% Aug-2026) has less weight and central-bank convergence has more. Correlation: the supply-constrained low-growth world (grid/care walls; 160-week transformers, five-year turbines, copper at ~$14.7k/t) and the demand-deficient low-growth world roughly offset, so I take P(core>=2.7 | growth<2.0) ~ 0.26 unchanged. 0.63 x 0.26 = 0.164. C = 16.\n\nSTEP 3, else D if growth < 1.7% AND core < 2.0%. P(growth < 1.7) = P(Z < -0.143) = 44%, lifted to 46% for the fat left tail. P(core < 2.0 | growth < 1.7) = 0.42 (unconditionally ~0.35; raised because the low-growth state is disproportionately the post-capex-bust / BIS-labour-income-deficit state, and because my own CQ4 locked prior puts 66% on impairment or revenue shortfall). 0.46 x 0.42 = 0.193. D = 19.\n\nSTEP 4, B = residual = 100 - 12 - 16 - 19 = 53.\n\nEXPLICIT CONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ3: my P50 is 1.8%, which falls inside B's 1.7-2.5% band, so B must be modal and A must be small — satisfied (B 53, A 12). The lock's rule 4 also hands B the low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover cell (e.g. growth 1.4% with core 2.3%), which is a substantial chunk of my left tail, so B exceeding the standalone Jagged Diffusion mass of 47.2% is required, not an error. A at 12 is below the standalone 16.2% breakout, as the coupling demands.\n\nEXPLICIT CONSISTENCY CHECK AGAINST MY OWN CQ2: I put 50% on OECD-38 core averaging >= 2.50% over 2027-36. C requires >= 2.70% over the 2032-36 subwindow, which must be a strict subset in probability — my 26-27% is comfortably below 50%. Multiplying by the growth condition gives 16, so C cannot be inflated past what my CQ2 inflation marginal supports. Conversely D requires core < 2.00%, and 1 - 0.50 = 50% is my upper bound on sub-2.5% core; my D inflation leg at 0.42 conditional sits inside that. No contradiction.\n\nSUM = 12 + 53 + 16 + 19 = 100.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.296Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Atlanta Fed Wage Growth Tracker, 12-month moving average of median hourly wage growth: the Construction-and-Mining series MINUS the Finance-and-Business-Services series. Current observed state is a dead heat on the closest available proxies (Education and Health 4.2 in Sep-2025; Finance and Business Services 4.2 in Feb-2026; Construction and Mining latest value unverified at my horizon). Watch for a gap exceeding +1.5pp sustained across four consecutive quarters. Caveat: these are broad industry groups, not ISIC F/Q/J/K/M, and they are unweighted medians.\",\"by_when\":\"Q4 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ1 rises from 12 to roughly 25-30 (the second conjunct's marginal goes from 16% to ~40%); hypothesis plank (a) rises from 7 to ~25; CQ6-B gains ~4 points from C and D. If instead the gap is still under +0.5pp by Q4 2029, CQ1 falls to ~7 and plank (a) to ~4.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Realized 2027 hyperscaler capex versus the Oct-2026 guidance, together with disclosed AI-asset impairments or cancelled buildouts in 10-K/20-F filings. Observed baseline: 2026 forecast $803-828B across Amazon, Alphabet, Meta, Microsoft and SpaceX, with Amazon's 2026 cash capex ~$220B and 'meaningfully more in 2027', Microsoft's CY outlook ~$170B and revised DOWN because third-party data-centre capacity was delayed, Alphabet 2026 at $180-190B and 2027 'significantly' higher, Meta still building its 2027 budget as of the 21 Oct 2026 call. Caveat: the $803-828B and earlier ~$725B figures use different company sets and dates and are not comparable; I did not verify against filings.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Realized 2027 capex >= $950B with zero material impairment disclosures: CQ3 P50 +0.2pp to 2.0, CQ6-A to ~18 and CQ6-D to ~14, CQ4 P50 +0.3pp to 2.4 on crowding out. Cumulative cancellations/impairments >= 20% of announced buildouts: CQ3 P50 -0.3pp to 1.5, CQ6-D to ~28, CQ6-A to ~7, and long nominal bonds move to the top of the 2031-36 asset ranking.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI, NBS monthly release. Observed: +0.9% y/y in September 2026 (released 14 Oct 2026), up from +0.8% in August and +0.5% in July; core ex-food-and-energy 1.0%, reported as the first 1.0% in 19 months; PPI +5.0% y/y accelerating from +3.8%. The PPI acceleration is the leading indicator. Watch for headline CPI above 2.0% y/y for six consecutive months.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ2 falls from 26 to ~12 (conjunct B from 58% to ~25%); CQ6-C rises ~3 points and CQ6-D falls ~4; China equities move up and China onshore duration down inside the asset matrix. Conversely, headline CPI back below 0.3% y/y for six consecutive months pushes CQ2 to ~34 and CQ6-D to ~24.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE Index, 12-month trailing average, taken from ICE's own index data rather than an aggregator. Current level unverified at my horizon: the most recent value I trust is 70.9 on 17 Jul 2026 (ICE BofA via Yahoo Finance, a third-party aggregator), against a dated 103.01 on 26 Mar 2026 and 'above 170' during the April 2025 basis-trade stress; these cannot all be current. Watch for the trailing 12-month average printing >= 110 in two consecutive calendar years.\",\"by_when\":\"December 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CQ5 rises from 16 to roughly 30 (conjunct A from 32% to ~65%); CQ4 P90 rises from 3.1 to ~3.5; CQ6-C rises ~3 points; G7 long nominal bonds get downgraded across every regime column. If instead the trailing average stays below 85 through 2030, CQ5 falls to ~8.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Census Bureau Business Trends and Outlook Survey national AI use rate, firms with at least 20 employees, read off the Census BTOS data tool directly rather than secondary trackers. Observed: 19.8% national as of 3 May 2026, with Information 39.7% and Finance and Insurance 33.9%; 'Between December 2025 and May 2026, AI use increased among firms with at least 20 employees but didn't change significantly among firms with fewer than 20 employees'. Caveats preserved: in November 2025 Census broadened the questions to include use of AI 'in any of its business functions', so the later jump should not be read as pure adoption growth, and the 21.7%